      "math_academy_id": "e-1385",
      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of  $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{8a}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we apply the quotient rule and put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{8a}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{8a}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to remove the radical $\\sqrt{8a}$ from the denominator. To do this, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{8a}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{8a}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{8a}}{\\sqrt{8a}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{24a}}{(\\sqrt{8a})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{4\\cdot 6a}}{8a} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{6a}}{8a} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6a}}{4a}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
